<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Giulio Alberoni</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1664">1664</a>–<a href="/years/1752">1752</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Giulio Alberoni was an Italian cardinal and statesman who, as chief minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-v-of-spain">Philip V of Spain</a> from 1715 to 1719, tried to restore Spanish power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and provoked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-quadruple-alliance">War of the Quadruple Alliance</a>. Dismissed and exiled in 1719, he later returned to papal service as legate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romagna">Romagna</a>. In 1739 he occupied San Marino for the Papal States, but the pope restored the republic's independence in February 1740.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/giulio-alberoni.jpg" alt="Giulio Alberoni" width="320" height="368" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Giovanni Maria delle Piane · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cardinale-Giulio-Alberoni_-_Giovanni_Maria_delle_Piane.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Alberoni was born near Piacenza in the Duchy of Parma, probably at Fiorenzuola d'Arda. His father, a gardener, died when he was ten, and he first served the church as bellringer and verger in the cathedral of Piacenza. He followed the banished judge Ignazio Gardini to Ravenna, where he met Giorgio Barni, who became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Piacenza in 1688 and made him chamberlain of his household. Ordained priest, he later became a canon of the cathedral of Parma.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a> the Duke of Parma sent him to Louis-Joseph, duc de Vendôme, commander of the French forces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. He went with Vendôme to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1706, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> received him well, and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> as his secretary, promoting the cause of Philip V.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After Vendôme's death in 1713 Alberoni became Parma's agent at Philip's court. When the queen, Maria Luisa of Savoy, died in 1714, he and the princesse des Ursins arranged the king's marriage to Elisabetta Farnese, daughter of the Duke of Parma, who then allied with Alberoni and banished the princess. By the end of 1715 he was a grandee, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Málaga and chief minister, and in July 1717 Pope Clement XI made him a cardinal.</p><p>Copying the reforms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-baptiste-colbert">Colbert</a>, he abolished internal customs houses, promoted trade with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, set up a regular mail service to the colonies and reorganised state finances. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> in 1717 and landed 38,000 troops on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> in 1718, but the Quadruple Alliance formed against it, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> destroyed a Spanish fleet off Cape Passaro, and his backing of a Jacobite landing and of the Cellamare conspiracy against the French regent failed. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> invaded and a British force took Vigo, he was dismissed on 5 December 1719.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Pursued by a commission of cardinals, Alberoni found refuge in Austrian Lombardy, took part in the conclave of 1721 and was briefly confined in a monastery before being cleared. Under Clement XII he served as legate at Ravenna, where he built the Porta Alberoni. At Piacenza he replaced the decayed leper hospital of San Lazzaro with a seminary for seventy poor boys, the Collegio Alberoni, and on his death on 26 June 1752 left 600,000 ducats to endow it. The college still holds his collections of Flemish tapestries and paintings, among them Antonello da <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/messina">Messina</a>'s Ecce Homo.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Giulio Alberoni” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>San Marino survived the occupation by <b>Cardinal Alberoni</b> in 1739–40, declined Napoleon's offer of more territory in 1797, sheltered Garibaldi in 1849 and was left independent at Italian unification.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-san-marino">Republic of San Marino</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Fiorenzuola d'Arda, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Piacenza, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.93,9.9&amp;year=1721" aria-label="Fiorenzuola d'Arda on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4786 577 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Giulio Alberoni"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5236 847h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5236 847h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.93,9.9&amp;year=1721">What was here in 1721? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
